  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "Does the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(5n)}{{n}^{5}}-1$ for $n\\ge 1$ converge or diverge? If the sequence converges, what is its limit?",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!\u226b{e}^{n}\u226b{n}^{m}\u226b\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $\u226b$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nWe calculate the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(5n)}{{n}^{5}}-1)=-1+{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(5n)}{{n}^{5}}\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(5n)}{{n}^{5}}=0.\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=-1+{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(5n)}{{n}^{5}}=-1+0=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence converges and its limit is $-1.$",
